schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Node founding-team roles: convener, builder/doer, treasurer-ops"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A minimal role model for a local node's founding team, filling jobs
    rather than titles — the convener (runs meetings, holds the calendar),
    the builder/doer (ships events/workshops/on-chain actions), and the
    treasurer/ops (holds the multisig, tracks grants, keeps the spreadsheet).
  source_traditions:
    - ReFi Medellín and ReFi Cartagena's real named team structures (cited as worked examples)
    - BanklessDAO's contributor playbook (specialize, delegate decisions)
    - Other Internet's "Web3 Work" research on DAO contributor burnout
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Presented as the antidote to "you don't need a CEO, you need a few jobs
    covered." The article's explicit rule: one person can hold two of the
    three roles at the start, but no one should hold all three for long, and
    ownership should be named out loud even informally.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - conventional startup C-suite roles (CEO/COO/CFO) — the article explicitly rejects this framing ("you do not need a CEO")
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      role clarity (naming who owns what) is treated as the cheapest
      burnout-prevention tool available, distinct from skill level or
      technical expertise.
  tensions:
    - >-
      fast bootstrapping (one person covering multiple roles early) vs.
      burnout risk and single-point-of-failure risk if that concentration
      persists.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating "founding team" as a headcount question rather than a coverage
    question misses the article's core claim: two people who trust each
    other and cover the three roles beats ten who attended one call.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/building-founding-team.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-f2680cdb2876
  source_lineage: building-founding-team.md
